</section>
<section id="A4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.3 </span>Framework Analysis</h3>

<section id="A4.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">D.3.1 </span>Ablation Study</h4>

<div id="A4.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To assess the effectiveness of the proposed memory trigger, we conduct an ablation study comparing different memory invocation strategies, as summarized in <a href="#A4.T5" title="In D.3.1 Ablation Study ‣ D.3 Framework Analysis ‣ Appendix D Extra Results ‣ MemGen: Weaving Generative Latent Memory for Self-Evolving Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. Specifically, <span id="A4.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Random</span> denotes a naïve baseline where latent memory tokens are inserted at arbitrary token positions with a fixed probability <math id="A4.SS3.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>. <span id="A4.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">All delimiters activated</span> represents a sentence-level strategy that invokes the memory weaver at every delimiter position without any selection mechanism. Finally, <span id="A4.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">MemGen</span><span id="A4.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">’s dedicated Trigger</span> corresponds to our standard approach, where the memory weaver is activated by the trained trigger <math id="A4.SS3.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{\text{trigger}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mtext>trigger</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{\text{trigger}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The results reveal several key observations. First, sentence-level intervention already improves performance compared to random invocation. For instance, activating the weaver at all delimiters yields <math id="A4.SS3.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="17.34\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>17.34</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">17.34\%</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A4.SS3.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="56.20\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>56.20</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">56.20\%</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A4.SS3.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="64.15\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>64.15</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">64.15\%</annotation></semantics></math> on GPQA, Kodcode, and TriviaQA, respectively, consistently outperforming all random baselines (e.g., <math id="A4.SS3.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.5</annotation></semantics></math> achieves only <math id="A4.SS3.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16.66\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>16.66</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16.66\%</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A4.SS3.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="52.95\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>52.95</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">52.95\%</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A4.SS3.SSS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="57.28\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>57.28</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">57.28\%</annotation></semantics></math>). This highlights the importance of aligning memory injection with semantic boundaries rather than distributing it across the sequence. More importantly, our trained trigger achieves the best overall performance, reaching <math id="A4.SS3.SSS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="18.28\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>18.28</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">18.28\%</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A4.SS3.SSS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="58.16\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>58.16</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">58.16\%</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A4.SS3.SSS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="65.02\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>65.02</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">65.02\%</annotation></semantics></math> on the three benchmarks. This demonstrates that selective activation, <span id="A4.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span>, deciding <span id="A4.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">when</span> and <span id="A4.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where</span> to weave in memory, provides the most effective support for reasoning, as it balances memory utility and interference more precisely than coarse-grained alternatives.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.T5" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 5: </span>Ablation study of different memory invocation strategies. Random denotes a naïve baseline where latent memory tokens are inserted at arbitrary token positions with a fixed probability <math id="A4.T5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>. All delimiters activated represents a sentence-level strategy that invokes the memory weaver at every delimiter position without any selection mechanism. Finally, <span id="A4.T5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">MemGen</span>’s dedicated Trigger corresponds to our standard approach, where the memory weaver is activated by the trained trigger <math id="A4.T5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{\text{trigger}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mtext>trigger</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{\text{trigger}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
