</section>
<section id="A4.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">D.3.2 </span>Analysis of Memory Weaver</h4>

<div id="A4.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We further investigate how the parameterization of the memory weaver influences downstream performance. As shown in <a href="#A4.T6" title="In D.3.2 Analysis of Memory Weaver ‣ D.3 Framework Analysis ‣ Appendix D Extra Results ‣ MemGen: Weaving Generative Latent Memory for Self-Evolving Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, increasing the number of trainable parameters by adopting a full-parameter SFT setup surely enhances the weaver’s memory and learning capacity, leading to higher task performance across benchmarks. Nevertheless, the LoRA-based instantiation already achieves competitive results while retaining high parameter efficiency, demonstrating that even lightweight adaptations can endow the weaver with sufficient capacity to generate effective latent memories.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.T6" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 6: </span>Ablation study of the latent weaver. We instantiate <span id="A4.T6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">MemGen</span> <sub id="A4.T6.5" class="ltx_sub">SFT</sub>’s memory weaver with two strategies: LoRA and full-parameter SFT.</figcaption>
<table id="A4.T6.6" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="A4.T6.6.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A4.T6.6.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt" style="padding:1.4pt 8.0pt;"><span id="A4.T6.6.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Base LLM: <span id="A4.T6.6.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct</span></span></td>
<td id="A4.T6.6.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding:1.4pt 8.0pt;"><span id="A4.T6.6.1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">GPQA</span></td>
<td id="A4.T6.6.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding:1.4pt 8.0pt;"><span id="A4.T6.6.1.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Kodcode</span></td>
<td id="A4.T6.6.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding:1.4pt 8.0pt;"><span id="A4.T6.6.1.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">TriviaQA</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A4.T6.6.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A4.T6.6.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t" style="padding:1.4pt 8.0pt;"><span id="A4.T6.6.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">LoRA (<math id="A4.T6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=16</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A4.T6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=32</annotation></semantics></math>)</span></td>
<td id="A4.T6.6.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:1.4pt 8.0pt;"><span id="A4.T6.6.2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">18.28</span></td>
<td id="A4.T6.6.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:1.4pt 8.0pt;"><span id="A4.T6.6.2.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">58.16</span></td>
<td id="A4.T6.6.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:1.4pt 8.0pt;"><span id="A4.T6.6.2.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">65.02</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A4.T6.6.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A4.T6.6.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb" style="padding:1.4pt 8.0pt;"><span id="A4.T6.6.3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Full SFT</span></td>
<td id="A4.T6.6.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding:1.4pt 8.0pt;"><span id="A4.T6.6.3.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">21.21</span></td>
<td id="A4.T6.6.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding:1.4pt 8.0pt;"><span id="A4.T6.6.3.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">60.00</span></td>
<td id="A4.T6.6.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding:1.4pt 8.0pt;"><span id="A4.T6.6.3.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">67.10</span></td></tr>
</table>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS3.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">D.3.3 </span>Efficiency Analysis</h4>

<div id="A4.SS3.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS3.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#A4.T7" title="In D.3.3 Efficiency Analysis ‣ D.3 Framework Analysis ‣ Appendix D Extra Results ‣ MemGen: Weaving Generative Latent Memory for Self-Evolving Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> reports average inference time and task performance across three benchmarks. Both SFT and <span id="A4.SS3.SSS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">MemGen</span> drastically reduce per-task inference time compared with vanilla models due to fewer tokens required to reach correct answers. For instance, on KodCode with <span id="A4.SS3.SSS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Qwen2.5-1.5B</span>, <span id="A4.SS3.SSS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">MemGen</span> <sub id="A4.SS3.SSS3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_sub">SFT</sub> completes tasks in <math id="A4.SS3.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.94" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2.94</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.94</annotation></semantics></math> s, a <math id="A4.SS3.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="75.4\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>75.4</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">75.4\%</annotation></semantics></math> reduction from <math id="A4.SS3.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="11.96" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>11.96</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11.96</annotation></semantics></math> s for vanilla, while improving accuracy by <math id="A4.SS3.SSS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="33.61\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>33.61</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">33.61\%</annotation></semantics></math>. On ALFWorld+<span id="A4.SS3.SSS3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Qwen3-8B</span>, <span id="A4.SS3.SSS3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">MemGen</span> <sub id="A4.SS3.SSS3.p1.1.7" class="ltx_sub">SFT</sub> adds only <math id="A4.SS3.SSS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.6\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.6</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.6\%</annotation></semantics></math> more time compared with SFT (<math id="A4.SS3.SSS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20.08" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>20.08</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20.08</annotation></semantics></math> s vs <math id="A4.SS3.SSS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="19.76" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>19.76</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">19.76</annotation></semantics></math> s) but increases accuracy from <math id="A4.SS3.SSS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="83.59\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>83.59</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">83.59\%</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A4.SS3.SSS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="85.82\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>85.82</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">85.82\%</annotation></semantics></math>. These results confirm that <span id="A4.SS3.SSS3.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">MemGen</span> efficiently enhances reasoning performance without incurring significant inference delays.</p>
</div>
